      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "half-diminished-ear-training",
      "narrative": "Fisher introduces the min7b5 (half-diminished) chord as a specific lick target requiring dedicated ear familiarization: 'There are many ways to improvise over min7b5 (half-diminished) chords. Here are just a few licks to help you get used to the sound.' The prescription is to learn the provided licks as an entry point into the unfamiliar sound of the chord before attempting broader substitution strategies. Chapter 4, p.72.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:lick-vocabulary-system:learn-transpose-apply",
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:lick-vocabulary-system:half-diminished-lick-family"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 4 p.72"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "i",
      "granularity_name": "other"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-upper-extension-via-maj7-arpeggio",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes playing a Major 7th arpeggio rooted on the flat-7 of the V7 chord to voice its upper extensions: 'Simply play the notes of a major 7th arpeggio rooted on the b7 degree of the V7 chord in any ii-V7-I progression. For example, if the V7 chord is G7, you can play an FMaj7 arpeggio because F is the b7 of the G7. Notice that this technique works nicely because you are actually just playing the upper extensions of the G7 chord: A is the 9th, C is the 11th and E is the 13th.' The arpeggio need not start on its own root. Chapter 3, p.49.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:ii-v7-i-harmonic-framework:maj7-from-b7-arpeggio-over-v7",
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:ii-v7-i-harmonic-framework:color-tone-upper-extensions"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.49"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-diminished-triad-substitution",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes substituting a diminished triad from the 3rd, 5th, or 7th of the V7 chord to produce altered extensions that resolve into the I chord: 'Play a diminished triad starting at the 3rd, 5th or 7th degree of the V7 chord in any ii-V7-I progression. For instance, if the V7 chord is G7, you can play a Bdim arpeggio, a Ddim arpeggio or an Fdim arpeggio. These tones will resolve nicely into the I chord.' The alteration produces a b9 and a b11 (enharmonic 10) among the extensions. Chapter 3, p.51.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:ii-v7-i-harmonic-framework:diminished-substitution-v7"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.51"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-ascending-lines-from-chord-tones",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes playing ascending scale-wise lines beginning on a chord tone of the V7, and alternating these with descending patterns for contour variety: 'Simply play ascending scale-wise patterns starting from chord tones' and 'Alternating ascending and descending patterns will give your line a more interesting contour.' The ascent must be anchored to a chord tone at its start. Chapter 3, p.53-55.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:ii-v7-i-harmonic-framework:ascending-lines-from-chord-tones"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
