      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "comping-no-double-with-keyboard",
      "narrative": "Fisher proscribes simultaneous free comping by both guitarist and keyboardist: 'When playing with a keyboard player, it is preferable to take turns comping behind the soloist. If both the guitarist and the keyboard player comp freely at the same time the tune will become like a harmonic can of worms.' The prescription is to alternate the comping role and always listen carefully to what is happening around you. Chapter 3, p.47.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:swing-comping-texture:no-double-comp-with-keyboard"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.47"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "polarity": "proscriptive",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "comping-swing-strum-technique",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes down strokes on downbeats and up strokes on upbeats for swing comping, with string damping during rests: 'It's a good idea to strum down on the downbeats and to strum up on the upbeats. This will help you keep your place in the measure. Also, try to damp the strings so that the chords don't sustain during the rests.' Damping is achieved by releasing left-hand pressure or stopping strings with the right-hand palm or wrist. Chapter 3, p.47.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:swing-comping-texture:damp-sustain-control"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.47"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "comping-swing-implied-rhythm",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes an improvisatory approach to swing comping where rhythm is implied rather than stated mechanically: 'Unlike rock, pop and country music, the rhythm in a swing feel is implied. The beat is often suggested by the guitarist. Predictable rhythm parts are considered to be trite, so the guitarist is left to accent various parts of each measure, usually with an element of surprise and with a spirit of improvisation.' The comping guitarist must vary accents unpredictably across the measure. Chapter 3, p.47.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:swing-comping-texture:swing-rhythm-implied-accent"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p.47"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "f",
      "granularity_name": "rhythmic-placement"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-rootless-voicing-three-string-sets",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes organizing V7 chord voicings (e.g., G9) across the three string sets (6-5-4-3, 5-4-3-2, 4-3-2-1) as rootless chords to permit more extensions: 'Playing rootless chords often permits the use of more extensions in chord voicings. The various voices can actually imply the sound of the root. Try to hear the root in your head when playing these chords.' In ensemble contexts the bass player provides the root. Chapter 3, p.56.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:ii-v7-i-harmonic-framework:root-fifth-omission",
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:ii-v7-i-harmonic-framework:string-set-shift"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
