      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "blues-i7-scalar-tool-selection",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes three interchangeable scalar tools for soloing over dominant 7th chords in blues contexts -- the minor pentatonic scale, blues scale, and Mixolydian mode: 'This lesson begins with a review of the minor pentatonic scale, blues scale and Mixolydian mode fingerings.' These are presented in C and must be transposed. Mature application combines these tools fluidly across the entire fingerboard rather than staying in a single position. Chapter 5, p.78-79.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:lick-vocabulary-system:blues-scale-tool-selection",
        "jody-fisher:jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate:lick-vocabulary-system:whole-neck-combination"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.78"
        },
        {
          "source": "jazz-guitar-method-vol-2-intermediate",
          "citation": "chapter 5 p.79"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "turnaround-v7-transposable-lick",
      "narrative": "Fisher prescribes creating and memorizing transposable licks specifically for V7 chords within turnaround contexts: 'Obviously, if turnarounds are common, you will need to know how to improvise your way through them. Create licks that you know will work over various turnarounds. This will help you make melodically smoother transitions back to the beginnings of your tunes. Memorize and transpose the ones you like.' Chapter 8, p.88.",
